<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dawit II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1496–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Dawit II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dawit II, also known as Lebna Dengel, was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in the early sixteenth century. His forces were repeatedly defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, most decisively at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-amba-sel">Battle of Amba Sel</a> in 1531, after which he was driven into flight. He died while the Ethiopian state was reduced to a remnant by the Adal conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dawit-ii.jpg" alt="Dawit II" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristofano dell'Altissimo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cristofano_dell%E2%80%99Altissimo,_Portrait_of_Lebn%C3%A4-Dengel._c._1552-1568.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Shimbra Kure in March 1529, Ahmad's army routed a much larger Ethiopian force under Emperor <b>Lebna Dengel</b>, shattering the military equilibrium that had defined the region for a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The existential test came in 1529 when Imam Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi, known as Ahmad Gran and armed with firearms obtained through Ottoman channels, inflicted a catastrophic defeat on <b>Lebna Dengel</b> at the Battle of Shimbra Kure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese priest Francisco Álvares, who came with the Portuguese embassy to <b>Dawit II</b> in the 1520s, went twice to see the churches and swore by God that all he wrote of them was true, and that there was much more than he had written.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The forces of Imam Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi of the Adal Sultanate fought the Ethiopians under <b>Emperor Dawit II</b> at Amba Sel, and Adal seized southern Ethiopia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Debre Damo, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Died in Debre Damo, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.37,39.29&amp;year=1524" aria-label="Debre Damo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5625 1629 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dawit II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6075 1899h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6075 1899h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.37,39.29&amp;year=1524">What was here in 1524? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
